import{_ as e,o as a,c as s,ag as o}from"./chunks/framework.BIas3y6W.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Safe Loop","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"resources/safe-loop.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"resources/safe-loop.md","filePath":"resources/safe-loop.md"}'),n={name:"resources/safe-loop.md"};function r(i,t,h,d,l,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[o(`<h1 id="the-safe-loop" tabindex="-1">The Safe Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-safe-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Safe Loop&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Print this page. Keep it next to your keyboard. Use it in every lab from week 3 onward.</p></blockquote><p>Vibe coding in its original form means accepting whatever the AI writes without reading it. That is fine for a throwaway toy. For anything you want to keep, share or trust, professional developers who work with AI converge on the same short loop. This course calls it <strong>the Safe Loop</strong>.</p><h2 id="the-six-steps" tabindex="-1">The six steps <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-six-steps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The six steps&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Safe Loop is six steps done in order. After step 6 you go back to step 1 for the next small change, so the steps form a circle: <strong>Describe → Plan → Step → Test → Read → Commit</strong>, then Describe again. Two habits sit inside the loop: <strong>check first</strong> (end of step 1) and <strong>teach back</strong> (end of step 5).</p><ol><li><strong>Describe.</strong> Say what you want: goal, context, constraints, and how you&#39;ll know it&#39;s done. Then <strong>check first</strong>: write down the check that proves it works (from week 6, an automated test where the logic allows it), in a separate prompt, before you ask for the code.</li><li><strong>Plan.</strong> Ask for a plan before any code, and edit it.</li><li><strong>Step.</strong> Ask for <strong>one</strong> small change.</li><li><strong>Test.</strong> Run it: your check, the normal case and at least one edge case.</li><li><strong>Read.</strong> Read the diff and keep only what you can explain. Then <strong>teach back</strong>: write 2–3 sentences in <code>PROMPTS.md</code> saying what changed and why.</li><li><strong>Commit.</strong> Make a save point with a message that says what changed, plus an <code>Assisted-by:</code> line if AI helped. Then go back to step 1.</li></ol><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Step</th><th>What you do</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Describe</strong></td><td>Write the prompt in four parts: <strong>Goal · Context · Constraints · Done when</strong>. Then <a href="#check-first">check first</a>: turn &quot;Done when&quot; into a check you can run, in a separate prompt, before asking for code.</td><td>The AI can only use what you tell it. &quot;Done when&quot; gives it (and you) a way to check, and a check written before the code isn&#39;t bent to fit the code.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Plan</strong></td><td>Ask for a plan <em>before</em> any code: &quot;Don&#39;t write code yet. Propose a plan in 3–6 steps.&quot; Edit the plan.</td><td>Fixing a plan costs seconds. Fixing code built on a bad plan costs hours.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Step</strong></td><td>Ask for <strong>one</strong> step of the plan.</td><td>Small changes are easy to test, read and undo. One giant prompt produces code you can&#39;t change later.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Test</strong></td><td>Run it. Run your check, then try the normal case <strong>and</strong> at least one edge case (empty, very long, reload, small screen). With an agent, make it run the tests and show you the output.</td><td>AI output is often &quot;almost right&quot;. Agents have claimed success while tests were failing.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Read</strong></td><td>Read the diff (what changed). Ask the AI to explain anything you don&#39;t understand. Keep only what you can explain. Then write a <a href="#teach-back">teach-back</a>.</td><td>&quot;If you can&#39;t explain it, you didn&#39;t build it.&quot; You will be asked to explain it.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Commit</strong></td><td>Make a git commit with a message that says what changed, e.g. <code>Add high score saved in localStorage</code>. If AI helped, add an <a href="#the-assisted-by-line"><code>Assisted-by:</code> line</a>.</td><td>A commit is a save point. If the next step goes wrong, you can go back.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="check-first" tabindex="-1">Check first <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-first" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check first&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What:</strong> write the check for a step <em>before</em> you ask the AI to build it, in a <strong>separate prompt</strong> (or a separate chat) from the one that writes the code.</p><ul><li><strong>Weeks 1–5:</strong> the check is the &quot;Done when&quot; line, written as things you will try by hand: &quot;Clicking Clear, then OK, empties the list. Reloading keeps it empty. Clicking Cancel changes nothing.&quot; Write it in your prompt or in your README&#39;s acceptance table before you ask for code. You write your first automated test in week 4, as practice.</li><li><strong>From week 6:</strong> the check is an <strong>automated test</strong> wherever the logic allows it: a small file in <code>tests/</code> that <code>npm test</code> (or <code>node --test</code>) runs. Write it yourself, or ask the AI for it in its own prompt that contains only the &quot;Done when&quot; line and the name of the function, not the code. Use the <a href="./prompt-patterns.html#16-check-first-test-prompt">check-first test prompt</a>. Things you can only judge by looking, such as a layout or a color, keep a written check you try by hand.</li><li><strong>Run the test before the code exists.</strong> It should fail (red). After the step, it should pass (green). This is <a href="./glossary.html#redgreen-testing">red/green testing</a>.</li><li><strong>Never let the AI change a test to make it pass.</strong> One developer reported a &quot;fix it&quot; prompt that changed a test&#39;s expected status code from 200 (success) to 500 (server error), so the broken code &quot;passed&quot; (<a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48662310" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hacker News</a>). If a test seems wrong, decide yourself, and say why in <code>PROMPTS.md</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Why:</strong> when an AI writes tests <em>after</em> it has written faulty code, the tests tend to agree with the faults. In one 2026 study, tests generated after buggy code caught 14% of the faults, against 25% for tests written independently (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05139" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Konstantinou et al.</a>). A check you write first, apart from the code, is a real check.</p><h2 id="teach-back" tabindex="-1">Teach back <a class="header-anchor" href="#teach-back" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Teach back&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What:</strong> before you commit AI-written code, write <strong>2–3 sentences</strong> in <code>PROMPTS.md</code>, in your own words: what changed, why it works, and what you&#39;d look at first if it broke. 🔴 AI off: write it yourself. A teach-back the AI wrote for you doesn&#39;t count (<a href="./../assessment/#ai-use-policy">AI use policy</a>).</p><div class="language-markdown vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">markdown</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-light-font-weight:bold;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;--shiki-dark-font-weight:bold;">**Teach-back:**</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> The agent added a </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\`clearAll()\`</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> function in app.js. It calls </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\`confirm()\`</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> first,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">then empties the </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\`todos\`</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> array and saves it to localStorage, and the list is redrawn from that</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">array, so an empty array means an empty list. If it broke, I&#39;d check the localStorage key is still &quot;todos&quot;.</span></span></code></pre></div><p>If you can&#39;t write the teach-back, you&#39;re not ready to commit: ask the AI to explain the diff (<a href="./prompt-patterns.html#5-explain-this-code">explain this code</a>), read it again, and then write it.</p><p><strong>When:</strong> for every AI-written change you keep. From week 11, a teach-back before <strong>every agent commit</strong> is part of the capstone <strong>Process</strong> grade (<a href="./../assessment/rubrics.html#capstone">rubric</a>). The <a href="./prompt-patterns.html#17-teach-back-check">teach-back prompt</a> can check your sentences against the diff <em>after</em> you&#39;ve written them.</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> in a 2026 study of 78 learners, 77% of those who had used AI without restrictions failed a 30-minute maintenance task once the AI was switched off, against 39% of those whose tool had required a short explanation before generated code could be added (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20206" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Sankaranarayanan</a>). The teach-back is that explanation step.</p><h2 id="the-assisted-by-line" tabindex="-1">The Assisted-by line <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-assisted-by-line" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Assisted-by line&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What:</strong> a commit made with AI help ends with a line naming the tool, separated from the message by a blank line:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Add clear-all button with confirmation</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Assisted-by: GitHub Copilot</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><p><strong>In the Source Control box</strong> (github.dev or a Codespace): type the message, press <strong>Enter</strong> twice to start a new paragraph, then type <code>Assisted-by: [tool]</code>. Commit as usual (the commit button, or <strong>Ctrl+Enter</strong> / <strong>Cmd+Enter</strong>).</p></li><li><p><strong>In the terminal</strong>, a second <code>-m</code> adds the line as its own paragraph:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> commit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -m</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Add clear-all button with confirmation&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -m</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;Assisted-by: GitHub Copilot&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p><strong>Check it:</strong> <code>git log -1</code> shows the last commit with its full message.</p></li></ul><p><strong>When:</strong> taught in week 3; required on AI-assisted commits from week 11. Name the tool you used (<code>Gemini</code>, <code>GitHub Copilot</code>, <code>Copilot CLI</code>). One line per tool if you used more than one.</p><p><strong>Why:</strong> it&#39;s how professionals disclose AI help. Open-source projects including LLVM, OpenStack, OpenTelemetry and Fedora use an <code>Assisted-by:</code> line, and about half of 281 open-source AI policies studied in 2026 require disclosure (<a href="https://allthingsopen.org/articles/open-source-ai-contributions-assisted-by-git-trailer-standard" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">All Things Open</a>; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07542" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hora et al.</a>). It also makes your own history searchable: <code>git log --grep &quot;Assisted-by&quot;</code>.</p><h2 id="the-two-strikes-rule" tabindex="-1">The two-strikes rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-two-strikes-rule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The two-strikes rule&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If <strong>two</strong> attempts to fix the same problem fail:</p><ol><li><strong>Stop.</strong> Don&#39;t paste the error a third time.</li><li>Go back to your last good commit if things got worse.</li><li>Start a <strong>fresh chat</strong> (long, muddled conversations make the AI worse).</li><li>Rewrite the prompt with what you learned: what you tried, the exact error, what you expected.</li></ol><h2 id="a-describe-step-example" tabindex="-1">A Describe-step example <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-describe-step-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A Describe-step example&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Goal: Add a &quot;clear all&quot; button to my to-do list.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Context: It&#39;s a single-page app in index.html, style.css and app.js. Items are saved in localStorage under the key &quot;todos&quot;.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Constraints: Plain JavaScript, no libraries. Ask me to confirm before clearing. Don&#39;t change any other feature.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Done when: Clicking the button, then confirming, empties the list; reloading the page shows it&#39;s still empty; cancelling leaves the list unchanged.</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="a-plan-step-example" tabindex="-1">A Plan-step example <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-plan-step-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A Plan-step example&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Don&#39;t write any code yet. Propose a plan in 3–5 small steps for the goal above.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>For each step, say which file changes and how I can check it worked.</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="how-the-loop-looks-from-chat-to-agent" tabindex="-1">How the loop looks from chat to agent <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-loop-looks-from-chat-to-agent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the loop looks from chat to agent&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The loop is the same all course. What changes is who types and where the check lives.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Weeks 1–5: chat assistant</th><th>Weeks 6–10: editor assistant (Copilot in a Codespace)</th><th>Weeks 11–16: agent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Describe</strong></td><td>You write the four-part prompt in the chat</td><td>Same, in Copilot Chat; attach the files the change touches</td><td>You write the prompt; <code>SPEC.md</code> and <code>AGENTS.md</code> add standing context. Both pass the <a href="./prompt-patterns.html#18-the-goldfish-test">Goldfish test</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Check first</strong></td><td>Write &quot;Done when&quot; as checks you&#39;ll try by hand</td><td>One automated test in <code>tests/</code> where the logic allows it, written in a separate prompt; run it and see it fail</td><td>The test is written first, in its own prompt or session. <code>AGENTS.md</code> says never to weaken or delete a test</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Plan</strong></td><td>&quot;Don&#39;t write code yet. Propose a plan.&quot;</td><td>Same, in Copilot Chat&#39;s Ask mode</td><td>Plan mode, or &quot;plan first and wait for my approval&quot;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Step</strong></td><td>Copy one step&#39;s code into your file</td><td>Accept one suggested edit</td><td>Approve one task. Approvals stay on; never autopilot or skip-permissions modes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Test</strong></td><td>Open the preview; try the normal case and an edge case</td><td>Run <code>npm test</code> or open the preview; try it by hand</td><td>The agent runs <code>npm test</code> and shows the output; <strong>you</strong> still try it by hand</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Read</strong></td><td>Read the code before pasting; ask &quot;explain this&quot;</td><td>Read the diff in Source Control</td><td>Read the diff in Source Control, or the pull request&#39;s <strong>Files changed</strong> tab</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Teach back</strong></td><td>2–3 sentences in <code>PROMPTS.md</code></td><td>2–3 sentences in <code>PROMPTS.md</code></td><td>2–3 sentences in <code>PROMPTS.md</code> before <strong>every</strong> agent commit (graded)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Commit</strong></td><td>Commit on github.com (week 2) or in github.dev (weeks 3–5); add <code>Assisted-by:</code> from week 3</td><td>Commit in Source Control or the terminal</td><td>Commit before <em>and</em> after every agent task; build each feature on a branch and merge it through a pull request (<a href="./git-cheatsheet.html#branches-and-pull-requests">how</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table>`,33)])])}const u=e(n,[["render",r]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
